# Scripts that run inside Linux containers MUST keep LF endings.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true on Windows -- the default for many installs -- a .sh
# file can be committed with CRLF, and then `#!/bin/sh\r` inside a container
# fails with "bad interpreter: no such file or directory". The error names the
# interpreter, not the line endings, so it is a genuinely unpleasant hour.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
*.template      text eol=lf

# Git hooks are shell scripts without a .sh extension, so the rule above does
# not reach them. Same failure, one step earlier: Git Bash would refuse the
# hook with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" on any Windows clone.
.githooks/*     text eol=lf
